\begin_layout Title
Asset Markets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 17, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Unlike other markets, each trader in a capital market may buy and sell assets
 in exchange for money.
 In addition, prices play an important role in capital markets as they provide
 an abundance of information, identifying endogenously formed determinants
 of supply and demand.
 Thus, the defining characteristic of experimental research on asset markets
 has been concered with the signficant informational role of prices under
 the conditions of asymmetric information.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Many of the early experiments with asset markets focused on the informational
 efficiency of markets.
 Plott and Sunder (1982) concldued that it is possible for markets to desseminat
e information from perfectly formed insiders to the uniformed.
 In addition, they compared the predictive efficiency of the rational expectatio
ns (RE) equilibrium hypothesis to the prior information (PI) equilibrium
 hypothesis.
 It was found that, given experience with replications, the behavior of
 markets converge to the predictions of the RE theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, Plott and Sunder (1988) tested the function of a market when there
 is less-than-perfect, diverse information among individual traders.
 This paper provides an excellent example of the interplay between theory
 and experimental data.
 There was no theoretical work suggesting imcomplete markets would have
 difficulty arriving at the RE equilibrium.
 However, it was soon discovered that the incomplete market was unable to
 aggregate information.
 When the information set available to traders was expanded, the market
 was able to reach the RE equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Along with informational efficiency, there have also been other important
 topics that have been a focus of asset market experimentation.
 Bubbles and false equilibria is one such topic where laboratory modeling
 is especially useful because it is difficult to address with field data.
 Smith, Suchanek, and Williams (1988) compared the alternative models of
 forecasting behavior and price adjustments between the REN and REM hypotheses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, a large amount of the research is concerned with learning and its
 dynamics in competitive markets.
 This type of research has proven to be very complex due to the other opportunit
ies to acquire knowledge, aside from the price, during trading.
 As a result, there is still much to be discovered and understood about
 the dynamics of information aggregation and dissemination.
\end_layout

